WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.417 --> 00:00:15.067
It was dark when the carriage arrived at Mrs.

2
00:00:15.167 --> 00:00:17.348
Errol's cottage near Dorincourt Castle.

3
00:00:18.429 --> 00:00:19.951
Cedric waited while Mr.

4
00:00:19.991 --> 00:00:22.232
Havisham helped his mother from the carriage.

5
00:00:23.233 --> 00:00:26.696
Then he followed them to the door, which was opened by a servant.

6
00:00:29.438 --> 00:00:31.200
Cedric looked around the main room.

7
00:00:32.672 --> 00:00:35.675
Two comfortable chairs sat on either side of the fireplace.

8
00:00:37.176 --> 00:00:38.717
Paintings covered the walls.

9
00:00:40.258 --> 00:00:43.381
Pretty, decorative objects sat on the mantel and the tables.

10
00:00:43.401 --> 00:00:48.845
"I'm glad you're going to live here," Cedric said to his mother.

11
00:00:48.865 --> 00:00:51.908
"This house is so big and comfortable!"

12
00:00:51.928 --> 00:00:57.072
"Yes, but it'll be different from our New York house."

13
00:00:57.693 --> 00:01:01.496
Mrs. Errol remembered sadly how Cedric's smile lit up that house.

14
00:01:02.776 --> 00:01:04.697
A white cat was sleeping in a chair.

15
00:01:06.277 --> 00:01:08.678
Cedric immediately began to make friends with it.

16
00:01:08.698 --> 00:01:15.860
"The housekeeper at the castle thought you might like a cat, ma'am," the servant said.

17
00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:18.300
"So you won't be lonely."

18
00:01:19.901 --> 00:01:21.941
Mrs. Errol's weary face brightened.

19
00:01:21.961 --> 00:01:26.102
"Oh, yes, a cat makes a house more home-like."

20
00:01:26.122 --> 00:01:31.864
"Dinner will be ready soon," the servant said, leaving the room.

21
00:01:33.632 --> 00:01:35.013
Mr. Havisham and Mrs.

22
00:01:35.153 --> 00:01:37.475
Errol stood talking quietly in a corner.

23
00:01:38.776 --> 00:01:41.778
Cedric was so busy playing with the cat that he didn't listen.

24
00:01:41.798 --> 00:01:44.920
"Cedric doesn't have to go tonight?"

25
00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:46.541
she said hopefully.

26
00:01:46.561 --> 00:01:49.243
"He'll stay with me one more night?"

27
00:01:49.263 --> 00:01:52.285
"That's correct," Mr.

28
00:01:52.305 --> 00:01:53.285
Havisham said.

29
00:01:53.305 --> 00:01:59.590
"After we've dined, I'll go to the castle and inform the earl of our arrival.

30
00:02:00.891 --> 00:02:02.792
Cedric will meet him tomorrow."

31
00:02:04.044 --> 00:02:07.045
Mrs. Errol looked fondly at her son sitting with the cat.

32
00:02:07.065 --> 00:02:11.587
"The earl doesn't know how much he's taking from me."

33
00:02:12.888 --> 00:02:14.769
She straightened up with a look of resolve.

34
00:02:14.789 --> 00:02:17.650
"Please tell him that I don't want the money."

35
00:02:19.851 --> 00:02:21.411
Mr. Havisham was puzzled.

36
00:02:21.431 --> 00:02:25.673
"You mean the monthly allowance he proposed to give you?"

37
00:02:25.693 --> 00:02:31.836
"Yes, I'm grateful for this house, because it means I can be near Cedric.

38
00:02:32.536 --> 00:02:34.597
But I have enough money of my own to live on.

39
00:02:35.458 --> 00:02:38.779
I'd rather not take the earl's money, because he dislikes me so."

40
00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:40.460
She shuddered.

41
00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:44.903
"I'd feel like he's bribing me to agree to this arrangement."

42
00:02:47.164 --> 00:02:48.825
Mr. Havisham rubbed his chin.

43
00:02:48.845 --> 00:02:52.547
"The earl's not going to like this."

44
00:02:53.687 --> 00:02:55.328
Mrs. Errol's face darkened.

45
00:02:55.348 --> 00:02:57.790
"He'll understand after a while.

46
00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:01.832
Why should I take money from the man who takes my child?"

47
00:03:03.892 --> 00:03:05.713
Mr. Havisham thought this over.

48
00:03:05.733 --> 00:03:08.514
"All right," he finally said.

49
00:03:08.534 --> 00:03:10.915
"I'll deliver your message."

50
00:03:10.935 --> 00:03:13.976
"Thank you," she said briskly.

51
00:03:16.077 --> 00:03:17.738
Later in the evening, Mr.

52
00:03:17.778 --> 00:03:19.338
Havisham arrived at the castle.

53
00:03:20.779 --> 00:03:25.321
A footman led him to the library, where the earl sat in an easy chair by the fire.

54
00:03:27.102 --> 00:03:30.443
His foot was propped up on a special stool to ease his gout pain.

55
00:03:30.463 --> 00:03:32.884
"Havisham, you're back."

56
00:03:34.248 --> 00:03:35.529
The earl looked around.

57
00:03:35.549 --> 00:03:38.652
"Where's the boy?"

58
00:03:38.672 --> 00:03:45.158
"He's spending one last night with his mother," the lawyer said as he sat down.

59
00:03:45.178 --> 00:03:48.261
"Go on," the earl prodded.

60
00:03:48.281 --> 00:03:50.923
"What sort of lad is he?"

61
00:03:52.064 --> 00:03:57.309
Mr. Havisham didn't give many details; he wanted the earl to make up his own mind about Cedric.

62
00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:05.150
"His lordship is a fine boy, but you'll find him different from most English children."

63
00:04:05.170 --> 00:04:07.471
"I'm sure."

64
00:04:08.291 --> 00:04:09.311
The earl snorted.

65
00:04:09.331 --> 00:04:14.814
"He's probably an obnoxious American brat with bad manners."

66
00:04:16.454 --> 00:04:19.316
Mr. Havisham rarely disagreed with his employer.

67
00:04:20.196 --> 00:04:23.657
It was an especially bad idea when the earl was suffering from gout.

68
00:04:25.618 --> 00:04:27.079
So there was a long silence.

69
00:04:27.999 --> 00:04:29.039
Finally Mr.

70
00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:32.917
Havisham said, "I have a message for you from Mrs.

71
00:04:33.077 --> 00:04:36.858
Errol." "I don't want any of her messages."

72
00:04:37.378 --> 00:04:38.458
The earl growled.

73
00:04:38.478 --> 00:04:41.179
"The less I hear from her, the better."

74
00:04:41.199 --> 00:04:45.400
"This is an important one," Mr.

75
00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:46.300
Havisham said.

76
00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:49.601
"She doesn't want any of your money."

77
00:04:51.342 --> 00:04:52.822
The earl leaned forward.

78
00:04:52.842 --> 00:04:55.403
"What did you say?"

79
00:04:55.423 --> 00:05:03.665
"She says she doesn't need the money, and as relations between you are strained—" "Strained?

80
00:05:04.386 --> 00:05:07.347
I'll say they're strained," the earl shouted.

81
00:05:07.367 --> 00:05:09.567
"I can't stand her!

82
00:05:10.588 --> 00:05:12.408
She's just a greedy American!"

83
00:05:13.708 --> 00:05:14.909
The lawyer shook his head.

84
00:05:14.929 --> 00:05:20.170
"You can hardly call her greedy when she's asked for nothing."

85
00:05:21.951 --> 00:05:24.792
The earl waved his hand to dismiss that argument.

86
00:05:26.068 --> 00:05:29.070
"She's just trying to trick me into visiting her.

87
00:05:30.410 --> 00:05:33.732
She thinks I'll admire her principles, but I won't!

88
00:05:34.913 --> 00:05:40.076
She'll soon be telling everyone she lives like a pauper because I don't give her any money.

89
00:05:41.176 --> 00:05:44.918
Well, she'll have that money whether she spends it or not!"

90
00:05:46.279 --> 00:05:48.940
The earl was breathless with anger and excitement.

91
00:05:52.962 --> 00:05:55.484
suppose she's turned the boy against me."

92
00:05:57.216 --> 00:05:59.878
"Actually she has another message for you.

93
00:06:00.879 --> 00:06:03.520
Please don't tell him that you hate her.

94
00:06:04.561 --> 00:06:07.583
She doesn't want to give him a reason to dislike you."

95
00:06:07.603 --> 00:06:12.907
"What?" The earl sank back in his chair in surprise.

96
00:06:12.927 --> 00:06:16.249
"You mean she hasn't told him?"

97
00:06:18.010 --> 00:06:19.651
Mr. Havisham shook his head.

98
00:06:19.671 --> 00:06:26.436
"Lord Fauntleroy's feelings about you will depend entirely on your actions."
